title The corrective queue emptied before the restart -- three reds repaired but never closed
prompt Permission to restart the outer recursion loop?
think A readiness question is answered by checking, not by feeling ready. Ran the sweep the loop itself would run on its first lap: working tree, remote parity, submodule initialisation, the ledger's open rows, and every guard a restarted loop leans on.
think Tree clean, both remotes at the same hash, all four vendored submodules initialised. Those were the easy ones and they passed.
think The ledger did not. Three rows -- 104, 105, 106 -- read as OPEN, because each was booked and repaired inside its own round yet none was ever given a closure note. That matters more than it looks: the loop's Standfast instruction says close the open agent-closable reds before taking new work, so a restart would have opened by re-fixing work already fixed, or by stopping the line for faults that no longer exist. Found it before saying yes rather than after.
think Wrote the three closures honestly rather than uniformly, because they did not all close the same way.
obs 105 and 106 close on witnesses, which is what reds-first asks. The recited crypto count is gone entirely and the guard now refuses any recited count with its RED path proven by planting one; the pin fault has a loom requiring the card's nib to name HEAD, its pre-amend sibling, or its parent, with the RED path proven twice.
obs 104 cannot close that way and the closure says so plainly. Reds-first holds that a fix closes on a witness on metal and never on a claim -- and read the seating before speaking about an ordering is a discipline rather than a predicate. No script distinguishes an ordering claim from an ordering measurement. So it closes by record, which is the weaker kind, and naming that is better than dressing a recorded lesson up as a proof. What stands in place of a witness is the seating being written and dated where anyone can read it, and the cost of not reading it now sitting in the ledger twice.
obs A closure note is not a new row -- the monotone witness welcomes it as the row it speaks about -- so the spine still reads 1..106 with no gaps and needed no count bump.
obs Every guard a restarted loop leans on runs GREEN: the nib loom, the seL4 userlevel licence sweep, the crypto count guard, the living-card ASCII witness, the REDS monotone spine, the recursion-prompts census, and the waymark registry seal.
obs The corrective queue is now genuinely empty, which is the honest precondition for a restart. A loop that wakes into an empty ledger climbs; one that wakes into three phantom reds stops.
file crux/REDS.md closure notes for 104, 105, and 106 -- two on witnesses, one on record with its weakness named
recommend keep-going The loop is clear to restart. Its route is the seated microkernel arc, whose Equinox 3 question is answered and whose Equinox 1 is unblocked by the granted fetch, so it wakes into real agent-doable work rather than a gate.
